      "estimatedMinutes": 30,
      "content": "<h2>What a COO Actually Does</h2><p>The title Chief Operating Officer is one of the most inconsistently defined roles in business. Ask ten CEOs what their COO does and you'll get ten different answers. Some COOs run every internal function and manage a team of hundreds. Others focus exclusively on strategy execution with no direct reports. Some are the CEO's number-two and heir apparent. Others are functional operators who will always be operators. This ambiguity is a feature - it reflects a role that adapts to the specific CEO, company stage, and strategic challenge needing to be solved.</p><p>What all COO roles share is a fundamental responsibility: translating strategic intent into operational reality. The CEO decides what the company should do. The COO figures out how to make it happen - with the right people, processes, systems, and cadence. When this works well, strategy moves from vision to execution at the speed the market demands. When it doesn't, the company produces beautiful plans that die in the gap between intention and action.</p><h2>The Six COO Archetypes</h2><p>Research by Nathan Bennett and Stephen Miles identified six distinct COO archetypes. The Executor implements strategy already defined - a highly structured operator who builds reliable delivery systems. The Change Agent drives turnarounds or transformations, with an explicit mandate to break what isn't working. The Mentor operates alongside a CEO who needs a seasoned operational partner complementing their strengths - common when a visionary founder needs operational depth they lack personally.</p><p>The Other Half is the CEO's complementary operational partner: CEO owns external, COO owns internal. The Partner is a genuine strategic co-leader involved in both strategy and operations equally. The CEO-in-Waiting is positioned as a successor in development, gaining the experience needed before ascending. Knowing which archetype you're hired as matters enormously - deploying the wrong archetype in the wrong context is one of the most common COO failure modes.</p><h2>Negotiating Your Mandate</h2><p>The most common COO failure mode isn't operational incompetence - it's mandate ambiguity. Before your first day, negotiate your mandate in writing: which functions you directly own, which you influence, what decisions you can make without CEO approval, your budget authority, and your role in the leadership team. A well-defined mandate is worth months of execution speed. The mandate conversation also reveals the CEO's underlying model of what they want - a strategic partner who pushes back, or a capable executor who asks few questions and delivers results. Neither is wrong; operating in the wrong mode for your CEO creates unnecessary friction.</p><h2>The COO Operating Rhythm</h2><p>Great COOs establish their operating rhythm in the first 30 days and protect it relentlessly. The rhythm includes: daily standing reviews of operational health metrics (15 minutes maximum), weekly leadership team sessions (90 minutes, structured agenda, documented decisions), monthly P&L reviews with functional leaders, and quarterly operating plan reviews with the full team. This cadence is the heartbeat of execution - miss it consistently and strategy drifts, accountability diffuses, and problems surface too late to address.</p>",
