      "estimatedMinutes": 30,
      "content": "<h2>Why an Operating Philosophy Is a Management Multiplier</h2><p>An operating philosophy is a precise, written description of how you make decisions, give and receive feedback, handle conflict, what you expect from people you work with, and what they can count on from you. It enables the organization to make autonomous decisions consistent with your judgment even when you're not in the room - compressing months of behavioral observation into days. Jeff Weiner's famous leadership principles document at LinkedIn produced an organization that could predict his thinking with high accuracy, raising decision quality across thousands of daily choices he'd never personally see.</p><h2>Five Domains to Address</h2><p>First: your decision framework - how you think under uncertainty, what you optimize for when values conflict, what process you expect for significant decisions. Be specific. 'I value speed over perfection in reversible decisions and bias toward action when the cost of being wrong is low' tells far more than 'I value decisiveness.' Second: your feedback model - how you give feedback, how you expect to receive it. If you give direct immediate feedback, people need to know that so they don't interpret directness as anger. Third: your conflict approach - what behaviors constitute legitimate dissent versus organizational noise, and what happens after a decision is made.</p><p>Fourth: your availability norms - how you want to be reached, at what urgency thresholds, and what constitutes appropriate escalation versus problems people should solve themselves. Setting these explicitly prevents both over-escalation that consumes your time and under-escalation that lets problems grow. Fifth: non-negotiables. Every COO has hard limits - behaviors or conduct they will not accept regardless of business results. If intellectual honesty is a hard limit, say it explicitly. Non-negotiables discovered through violation are organizationally expensive; stated in advance, they're simply clear parameters.</p><h2>Accountability in Your Philosophy</h2><p>Your operating philosophy should explicitly address accountability - one of the most misunderstood concepts in operational leadership. Accountability is not blame assignment after something goes wrong. It is the proactive commitment of one person to one outcome, made public before execution begins. Three elements required: a specific measurable outcome one named person owns, a defined time horizon, and clarity on what help they have access to. Many COOs confuse responsibility (doing work) with accountability (owning results). A team can be collectively responsible for a project. Only one person can be accountable for its outcome. When multiple people are accountable, nobody is.</p>",
